{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788264663,"nonce":"uQawQQfCMRXNiK/T5szbz2ynh+0=","message":"SCOPE EXTENSION — OUT-3 of the M1 post-mortem (`RUNS/m1-20260831/postmortem.md`). The doctor-coverage read in the body above is half of a procedure; this is the other half, and it belongs here because it shares `PawnManageVerbs.cs` and the same roster-change trigger.\n\n**The casualty-triage procedure.** Three parts, of which the body already covers the first:\n\n1. **≥2 doctors** — the `work_coverage` read and the roster-change repair, as specified above.\n2. **Forced `rescue`.** This is the sharpest single finding in the whole post-mortem. At tick 231,968, with Captain bleeding out 118 cells away, the fix attempted was a **work-priority flip** — an adjustment to what a pawn might choose to do next. Meanwhile `rescue` is SHIPPED, it FORCES the job, and it INTERRUPTS `LayDown`. It was called **zero times in 195 ops.** The verb that solves this exists and the run never reached for it. A triage procedure that names `rescue` as the response to a downed colonist removes the entire class of \"adjust priorities and hope\".\n3. **Refuse to advance when the bleed clock is shorter than the travel time.** The decision at 231,968 was arithmetic: a ~9,040-tick bleed clock against a ~2,810-tick walk. Both numbers were computable and neither was published. `61794cd` publishes `ticks_until_bleedout`; this procedure is what consumes it. When the clock is shorter than the nearest capable rescuer's travel, advancing is a decision to let the pawn die and should have to be made explicitly.\n\nPart 3 depends on `61794cd` landing first. Parts 1 and 2 do not.\n\nNote the interaction with `722c951`: that issue halts the advance ON a casualty. This one decides what to DO about one. They should not each grow their own half of the other.\n\nAdditional acceptance:\n- A downed own-faction colonist with a capable rescuer available causes a forced `rescue`, journalled, not a priority change.\n- No capable rescuer: the procedure says so explicitly rather than doing nothing.\n- With `61794cd` landed: an advance is refused when the nearest rescuer's travel exceeds the bleed clock, and the refusal reports both numbers.\n- Replay tick 231,968 from `RUNS/m1-20260831/` and show the procedure reaches for `rescue`.","files":null}]}